1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,560
Fold a sheet of paper in half forty two

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:03,680
times, and mathematically, it would be

3
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:05,250
thicker than the distance to the Moon.

4
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:05,530
Sounds impossible?

5
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:06,640
The math checks out.

6
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:08,710
Every fold doubles the thickness.

7
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:11,040
Here's the math, and why engineers take

8
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,960
doubling seriously.

9
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,520
Let's follow the folds, one doubling at a

10
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:15,990
time.

11
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Start with an ordinary sheet of printer

12
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
paper. It's about a tenth of a millimeter

13
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,560
thick. Fold it in half once, and now you

14
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:23,600
have two layers.

15
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,760
Fold again, and you have four layers.

16
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:27,120
Again, eight.

17
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:29,520
Every fold doubles the number of layers,

18
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:31,070
so every fold doubles the thickness.

19
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:32,002
In this ideal version, we assume the paper

20
00:00:32,012 --> 00:00:32,045
never gets squashed, so every layer stays

21
00:00:32,055 --> 00:00:33,200
a tenth of a millimeter thick.

22
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:34,640
Let's write it down.

23
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:37,440
Zero folds, zero point one millimeters.

24
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:39,520
One fold, zero point two.

25
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:39,759
Then zero point four, zero point eight,

26
00:00:39,769 --> 00:00:43,120
one point six, three point

27
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:45,440
two, and six point four.

28
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:47,190
At fold seven, the stack is twelve point

29
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,520
eight millimeters, thicker than most

30
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:51,830
phones. At fold ten, it's one hundred and

31
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:53,600
two millimeters.

32
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,360
That's about ten centimeters, the height

33
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:56,480
of a coffee mug.

34
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:58,560
Ten folds made it about a thousand times

35
00:00:58,560 --> 00:00:59,280
thicker.

36
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:01,590
Here's the pattern as a formula.

37
00:01:01,590 --> 00:01:03,670
Call the starting thickness t.

38
00:01:03,670 --> 00:01:06,870
After n folds, the thickness T is t times

39
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:08,310
two to the n.

40
00:01:08,310 --> 00:01:10,070
Now notice something handy.

41
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:11,420
Two to the ten is one thousand and twenty

42
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:14,870
four. That's almost exactly a thousand.

43
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:16,870
So every ten folds multiplies the

44
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:18,550
thickness by about a thousand.

45
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:20,710
Ten folds, about ten centimeters.

46
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:22,950
Twenty folds, about a hundred meters.

47
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:25,830
Thirty folds, about a hundred kilometers.

48
00:01:25,830 --> 00:01:27,527
Now compare two ways to grow.

49
00:01:27,537 --> 00:01:28,060
Both start with one sheet.

50
00:01:28,070 --> 00:01:30,790
The blue line adds one new sheet at every

51
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:32,790
step. That's linear growth.

52
00:01:32,790 --> 00:01:34,550
The red curve doubles the sheets at every

53
00:01:34,550 --> 00:01:36,950
step. That's exponential growth.

54
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:39,110
For the first few steps, they look almost

55
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:42,550
the same. But after ten steps, the blue

56
00:01:42,550 --> 00:01:43,100
stack has eleven sheets, just one point

57
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:45,110
one millimeters thick.

58
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:46,014
The red stack has one thousand and twenty

59
00:01:46,024 --> 00:01:47,830
four sheets, over a hundred millimeters.

60
00:01:47,830 --> 00:01:49,750
Adding is slow and steady.

61
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:51,910
Doubling runs away.

62
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:53,750
Keep folding, and switch the scale to

63
00:01:53,750 --> 00:01:56,070
kilometers. For about twenty folds, the

64
00:01:56,070 --> 00:01:57,590
curve hugs the floor.

65
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:59,120
By then the stack is about a hundred

66
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:00,880
meters, as tall as a thirty storey

67
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:03,120
building. Three folds later, it's taller

68
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:05,120
than the Burj Khalifa, the tallest

69
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:06,320
building in the world.

70
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:08,800
At fold twenty seven, it's thirteen

71
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,480
kilometers. That's higher than Mount

72
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:12,960
Everest, and higher than airliners fly.

73
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:14,550
At fold thirty, it's about a hundred and

74
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:16,000
seven kilometers.

75
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:17,323
That's past the Kármán line, the commonly

76
00:02:17,333 --> 00:02:19,200
used boundary of space.

77
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,200
Now picture the stack in thousands of

78
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:22,870
kilometers. The Moon is about three

79
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:24,560
hundred and eighty four thousand

80
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:25,760
kilometers away.

81
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:27,686
At fold forty, the stack is about a

82
00:02:27,696 --> 00:02:29,760
hundred and ten thousand kilometers.

83
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:31,200
At fold forty one, two hundred and twenty

84
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:34,480
thousand. Still only partway there.

85
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:37,120
Then fold forty two doubles it again, to

86
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:38,800
about four hundred and forty thousand

87
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,670
kilometers. You've passed the Moon.

88
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,800
And here's the astonishing part.

89
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:42,828
The final fold alone added more than two

90
00:02:42,838 --> 00:02:42,851
hundred thousand kilometers, as much

91
00:02:42,861 --> 00:02:43,830
thickness as all forty one previous folds

92
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:44,230
combined.

93
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:45,920
We can also run it backwards.

94
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:47,760
How many folds does it take to reach the

95
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:50,960
Moon? Set t times two to the n equal to

96
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:51,680
the distance.

97
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:54,080
Put both in meters, and divide.

98
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:56,470
That gives two to the n equals about three

99
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:59,520
point eight four times ten to the twelve.

100
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:01,440
To solve for an exponent, you use a

101
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:04,280
logarithm. Log base two of that number is

102
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:05,910
forty one point eight.

103
00:03:05,910 --> 00:03:08,070
You can't make eight tenths of a fold, so

104
00:03:08,070 --> 00:03:10,630
round up. Forty two folds.

105
00:03:10,630 --> 00:03:12,620
Why can't you actually fold paper forty

106
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:15,340
two times? Because each fold makes the

107
00:03:15,350 --> 00:03:18,310
stack thicker while reducing the area

108
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:19,910
available for the next fold.

109
00:03:19,910 --> 00:03:23,190
After seven folds, an ordinary sheet could

110
00:03:23,190 --> 00:03:25,670
already be more than a centimeter thick,

111
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:27,110
with only a tiny footprint remaining.

112
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:28,790
At that point, bending the growing stack

113
00:03:28,790 --> 00:03:30,070
becomes extremely difficult.

114
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:31,590
That's why most people can only manage

115
00:03:31,590 --> 00:03:32,716
around six or seven folds with ordinary

116
00:03:32,726 --> 00:03:33,100
printer paper.

117
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:34,470
For years, people commonly believed you

118
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:35,430
couldn't fold paper in half more than

119
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:37,590
seven times. Then in two thousand and one,

120
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:39,590
a California high school student named

121
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:42,220
Britney Gallivan was challenged by her

122
00:03:42,230 --> 00:03:43,750
math teacher to fold something in half

123
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:46,870
twelve times for extra credit.

124
00:03:46,950 --> 00:03:48,710
In January two thousand and two, she

125
00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:50,540
folded a strip of toilet paper about one

126
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:53,590
point two kilometers long in half twelve

127
00:03:53,590 --> 00:03:55,320
times, all in the same direction.

128
00:03:55,330 --> 00:03:57,380
Gallivan also derived a formula for the

129
00:03:57,390 --> 00:03:59,030
minimum length of paper needed when every

130
00:03:59,270 --> 00:03:59,970
fold goes in

131
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:07,980
the same direction.

132
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:09,490
The equation shows that the required

133
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:11,980
length grows roughly fourfold with every

134
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:12,780
additional fold.

135
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:18,220
For forty two folds of paper one tenth of

136
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:18,270
a millimeter thick, the theoretical length

137
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:20,060
would be more than ten to the twenty one

138
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:21,660
meters. That's approximately one hundred

139
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:23,154
thousand light years, comparable to the

140
00:04:23,164 --> 00:04:23,810
diameter of the Milky Way.

141
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:23,854
Of course, this is a mathematical

142
00:04:23,864 --> 00:04:23,890
extrapolation, not something we could

143
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:25,260
physically build.

144
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:27,180
This isn't just a party trick.

145
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:29,660
Engineers run into doubling all the time.

146
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:31,580
For decades, the number of transistors on

147
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:33,420
a computer chip doubled roughly every two

148
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:33,785
years. That's Moore's law, an industry

149
00:04:33,795 --> 00:04:34,130
trend rather than a law of physics.

150
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:36,743
In a nuclear reactor, neutrons released

151
00:04:36,753 --> 00:04:38,220
when an atom splits can go on to split

152
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:39,900
more atoms, so the chain reaction has to

153
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:41,340
be carefully controlled.

154
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:43,330
And money growing at seven percent a year,

155
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:44,050
with compound interest, doubles in a

156
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:45,420
little over ten years.

157
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:52,460
So here's the one thing to remember.

158
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:55,020
Ten doublings is about a thousand times.

159
00:04:55,020 --> 00:04:57,100
Forty two doublings turn a tenth of a

160
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:57,970
millimeter into four hundred and forty

161
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:00,460
thousand kilometers.

162
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:02,380
That's how a sheet of paper, at least on

163
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:04,310
paper, reaches the Moon.

164
00:05:04,310 --> 00:05:06,150
If you enjoyed this one, please give it a

165
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:07,910
like and subscribe for more everyday

166
00:05:07,910 --> 00:05:09,590
engineering. Thanks for watching!
